15
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:26,033
A STAR IS BORN
Scene 1

16
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:28,066
FADE IN:
MOONLIGHT. LONG SHOT EXPANSE OF SNOW.

17
00:01:28,133 --> 00:01:31,066
In the foreground a wolf silhouetted in the moonlight.

18
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:34,033
In the background the isolated farmhouse of the Blodgetts.

19
00:01:34,166 --> 00:01:37,466
As we hear the melancholy howling of the wolf, we --
DISVOLVE TO:

20
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:44,330
(Howling)

21
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:49,800
Well, home from the movies at last.

22
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:51,200
Looks like it, Aunt Mattle.

23
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:52,866
Hi, Dad!

24
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:54,000
Hello, Son!

25
00:01:55,033 --> 00:01:57,400
Well, daughter, how was the movie pictured tonight?

26
00:01:57,600 --> 00:01:59,200
Hmm.
Lovely.

27
00:01:59,933 --> 00:02:02,200
Right, that's what it was. Just a lot of mud.

28
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:04,200
There wasn't anybody killed in the whole thing.

29
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:07,100
No, well then, I'll stick to these, these don't talk.

30
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:10,133
Well, that big clock, Norman Maine was in the pictures tonight,

31
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:12,500
he never does anything but kiss a lot of girls.

32
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:14,933
Norman Maine, is one of the best actors in pictures.

33
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:17,300
You and your movies.
That's all that you think about.

34
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:20,400
You shouldn't be allowed to go to them at all, if you ask me.

35
00:02:20,966 --> 00:02:23,100
Too bad I was so busy in the kitchen.

36
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,300
And I didn't hear anybody asking you.

37
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:27,266
Hello, Granny!

38
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:28,366
Hello, Darling

39
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:30,266
And off course no one ever listens to me!

40
00:02:30,433 --> 00:02:32,333
They do if they're within ten miles of ya.

41
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:33,933
Gathering around picture shows,

42
00:02:34,266 --> 00:02:36,233
house all covered up with movies magazines.

43
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:39,566
And the other day I caught her talking to a horse with a Swedish accent!

44
00:02:39,633 --> 00:02:40,600
Well, Sis...

45
00:02:41,766 --> 00:02:43,400
...you're only young once, you know?

46
00:02:45,766 --> 00:02:46,666
Ah!

47
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:48,500
Hollywood!

48
00:02:49,283 --> 00:02:52,575
You'd better be getting yourself a good husband and stop mooning about Hollywood.

49
00:02:53,100 --> 00:02:54,300
You know what she wants to do?

50
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:56,400
She wants to go to Hollywood!

51
00:02:57,566 --> 00:02:59,333
I've noted all along.

52
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:02,766
I've seen her making faces in the mirror and talking to herself

53
00:03:03,366 --> 00:03:05,000
That's what comes of your movies!

54
00:03:06,166 --> 00:03:09,166
Why? What would you do if you did go to Hollywood?

55
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:10,700
I'd be an actress.

56
00:03:10,833 --> 00:03:11,733
Ha, ha, ha, ha.

57
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:13,900
I would, I tell ya, I've always known I could.

58
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:19,633
Guys, wouldn't it be wonderful to have a movie star in the family?
Oh, Miss Blodgett, may I have your autograph?

59
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:23,200
You may not know it Aleck, but you're practically on your way to bed.

60
00:03:23,233 --> 00:03:27,100
Oh, Miss Blodgett you're my favorite actress, wouldn't you tell me the secret of your success?

61
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:28,000
Oh, let me alone!

62
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:30,466
Why Esther, what's come over you?

63
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:32,100
I'll tell you what's come over her.

64
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:35,700
She's just a silly little girl whose head is been turned by the movies

65
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:38,600
And as soon as she forgets the whole thing, the better off she'll be.

66
00:03:38,700 --> 00:03:40,100
Oh, why will I be better off?

67
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:44,900
What's wrong with want to go out and make something of myself? What do you do that's so much better?

68
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,600
Just because you're satisfied to sit here all your life, you think you can laugh at me!

69
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:53,300
Well, some day you won't laugh at me! I'm going out and have a real life! I'm gonna be somebody!

70
00:03:57,933 --> 00:04:02,600
Now if it was spring, I'd say give her a good dose of sulfur and molasses.

71
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:09,266
I thought I'd find you up.

72
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:11,600
Oh, stop that!

73
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:14,000
Now stop crying! that isn't going to do you a bit good.

74
00:04:14,133 --> 00:04:17,233
I'm crying because Aunt Mattie and Aleck make so mad.

75
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:21,300
Well, it's your aunt Mattie, Aleck.
They're not important.

76
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,066
You're the only one that counts.

77
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:27,966
Esther, everyone in this world who has ever dreamed about better things

78
00:04:28,075 --> 00:04:30,575
has been laughed at, don't you know that?

79
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:32,200
Oh, I suppose I do, but --

80
00:04:32,433 --> 00:04:34,866
But there's a difference between dreaming and doing.

81
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:37,366
The dreamers just sit around and moon

82
00:04:37,433 --> 00:04:39,500
about how wonderful it would be

83
00:04:39,533 --> 00:04:41,633
if only things were different.

84
00:04:42,700 --> 00:04:44,800
And the years roll on and they grow old

85
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:47,666
and by and by they forget everything,

86
00:04:48,266 --> 00:04:49,900
even about their dreams.

87
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:52,900
I don't want to be like that,
I want to be somebody,

88
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:53,966
Oh yes, oh yes.

89
00:04:54,066 --> 00:04:55,333
You want to be somebody,

90
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:56,800
but you want it to be easy.

91
00:04:57,066 --> 00:04:59,600
Oh, you modern girls give me a pain!

92
00:05:00,400 --> 00:05:01,766
When I wanted something better,

93
00:05:01,966 --> 00:05:05,900
I came across those planes in the prairies... with your Grandfather.

94
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:09,866
Oh, everyone laughed at us, like they did with the other pioneers.

95
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,700
They said this country will never be anything but a wilderness.

96
00:05:13,242 --> 00:05:14,783
We didn't believe that.

97
00:05:15,100 --> 00:05:17,100
We were going to make a new country.

98
00:05:18,033 --> 00:05:18,600
Besides,

99
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:22,800
we wanted to see our dreams come true.

100
00:05:23,033 --> 00:05:24,766
Oh Granny, it must have been wonderful.

101
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:26,266
It was wonderful.

102
00:05:26,766 --> 00:05:30,566
But don't you think for one single minute that it was easy, Esther Blodgett.

103
00:05:31,533 --> 00:05:34,242
We burned in summer and we froze in winter.

104
00:05:34,466 --> 00:05:37,100
But we kept right on going and we didn't complain.

105
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:39,466
Because we were doing what we wanted to do!

106
00:05:40,533 --> 00:05:41,733
Can you understand that?

107
00:05:44,166 --> 00:05:45,133
Yes, I can.

108
00:05:47,100 --> 00:05:48,200
Could you do it?

109
00:05:49,633 --> 00:05:51,800
Could you do it even if it broke your heart?

110
00:05:52,700 --> 00:05:53,733
Because remember Esther,

111
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:55,933
for every dream of yours you make come true,

112
00:05:56,066 --> 00:05:58,400
you'll pay the price in heartbreak.

113
00:06:01,533 --> 00:06:03,600
I know what I'm talking about.

114
00:06:04,758 --> 00:06:05,950
You may not believe it,

115
00:06:06,742 --> 00:06:08,242
but I was a young girl once.

116
00:06:09,123 --> 00:06:10,609
A very pretty young girl,

117
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:12,300
a lot prettier than you are.

118
00:06:13,966 --> 00:06:15,866
I was in love with your grandfather,

119
00:06:16,566 --> 00:06:18,933
and when some indian devil put a bullet through him,

120
00:06:19,633 --> 00:06:22,266
I felt like it had come straight through my heart too.

121
00:06:23,100 --> 00:06:24,666
I remembered all he taught me

122
00:06:25,470 --> 00:06:26,956
and I went right on him.

123
00:06:27,333 --> 00:06:29,633
I buried him out there on that wilderness

124
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:30,600
with my own hands.

125
00:06:32,033 --> 00:06:33,666
I went right on that same day

126
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:37,000
And I kept right on the way,

127
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:39,200
even when your mother was born.

128
00:06:40,466 --> 00:06:44,166
Oh Granny, I would make it worthwhile.

129
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:49,266
You know, Esther,

130
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:51,633
there'll always be a wilderness to conquer.

131
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:54,833
Maybe Hollywood is your wilderness now.

132
00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:56,800
From all I hear,

133
00:06:57,400 --> 00:06:58,233
it sounds like it.

134
00:06:59,766 --> 00:07:02,300
But if you've got one drop of my blood in your veins,

135
00:07:03,166 --> 00:07:06,466
you won't let Mattie or any of her kind break your heart,

136
00:07:07,166 --> 00:07:09,800
you'll go right out there and break it yourself.

137
00:07:10,533 --> 00:07:11,500
That's your right.

138
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:15,466
Here.

139
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:17,533
Oh, here, here, stop that nonsense.

140
00:07:18,866 --> 00:07:19,200
Here.

141
00:07:20,300 --> 00:07:21,433
Take this and go to your Hollywood.

142
00:07:21,900 --> 00:07:23,200
Oh, I can't take you money.

143
00:07:23,300 --> 00:07:24,066
Well, why not?

144
00:07:24,133 --> 00:07:25,100
it's your savings!

145
00:07:25,366 --> 00:07:27,200
Well, I was only saving up for my funeral.

146
00:07:27,733 --> 00:07:29,566
Now I don't think I'm ever going to die.

147
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:32,266
Oh, granny, how can I ever thank you?

148
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:35,600
By giving me your word of honor that you'll never tell a living soul

149
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:37,100
where you got that money.

150
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:38,033
I promise.

151
00:07:38,433 --> 00:07:40,433
Remember,
if you do,

152
00:07:41,333 --> 00:07:42,833
I'll have you arrested for robbing me.

153
00:07:51,900 --> 00:07:53,200
Here we are.

154
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:54,600
Want me to help you, Granny?

155
00:07:54,833 --> 00:07:55,900
Oh, I can make it, Dear.

156
00:07:57,633 --> 00:07:58,200
Oh!

157
00:07:58,400 --> 00:08:00,400
Man, it's cold, uh.

158
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:03,733
I kissed Dad goodbye,
just a little kiss, he didn't even wake up.

159
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:05,400
I bet you didn't try that on your Aunt Mattie.

160
00:08:05,600 --> 00:08:06,633
Oh, Aunt Mattie...

161
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:08,033
Think of her face when you tell her.

162
00:08:08,100 --> 00:08:09,400
I am thinking about it.

163
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:12,300
I've waited for that chance for thirty years.

164
00:08:13,133 --> 00:08:14,300
Fresh air

165
00:08:14,433 --> 00:08:16,633
Oh, thank you Grelsy.
Girls, eh?

166
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:23,066
Come on, come on!

167
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:25,566
Look, there is you train.

168
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:38,733
All aboard!

169
00:08:39,433 --> 00:08:40,666
Oh, Granny!

170
00:08:42,333 --> 00:08:44,933
Come on, come on! go on! go on!

171
00:08:46,950 --> 00:08:47,950
Good bye Granny!

172
00:08:48,200 --> 00:08:49,166
Good bye!

173
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:51,800
I'll be waiting for you in those movies pictures.

174
00:08:52,700 --> 00:08:53,700
And don't tell Mattie,

175
00:08:54,233 --> 00:08:56,400
you know my eyes are not as good as they used to be,

176
00:08:56,866 --> 00:08:58,200
But my ears are alright.

177
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:01,100
So you remember, talk up good and loud.

178
00:09:03,800 --> 00:09:04,933
All aboard!

179
00:09:06,233 --> 00:09:06,900
Goodbye!

180
00:09:07,700 --> 00:09:08,333
Goodbye!

181
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:12,200
Goodbye Granny!

182
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:14,633
Goodbye.

183
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:21,066
Do you want to go home now?

184
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:23,566
I don't want to

185
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:25,966
but I will.

186
00:09:31,533 --> 00:09:35,133
HOLLYWOOD
...the beckoning El Dorado...

187
00:09:35,217 --> 00:09:39,000
Metropolis of Make-Believe in the Californian hills.

188
00:11:05,500 --> 00:11:07,433
ROOMS FOR RENT
Hollywood

189
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:09,333
$6 WEEKLY

190
00:11:09,400 --> 00:11:11,800
Large rooms, running water.

191
00:11:11,900 --> 00:11:13,400
No cowboys.

192
00:11:13,400 --> 00:11:15,200
Convenient to all studios.

193
00:11:27,966 --> 00:11:28,833
Good Afternoon.

194
00:11:30,608 --> 00:11:31,942
Day, week or month?

195
00:11:32,608 --> 00:11:34,442
But it's a little hard to say you see,

196
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:35,900
I'm going into the movies.

197
00:11:36,100 --> 00:11:37,433
Well you better take it for a week,

198
00:11:37,500 --> 00:11:39,600
it'll break your jump to Beverly Hills.

199
00:11:40,033 --> 00:11:41,700
Are all the studios really near here?

200
00:11:42,900 --> 00:11:44,766
All except Gramon British.

201
00:11:45,525 --> 00:11:48,775
I suppose the best way to get a job is to go straight to the studios, isn't it?

202
00:11:49,900 --> 00:11:52,300
I have many illusions you know, I am perfectly willing to begin with...

203
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:53,566
ooh a little bit of a part,

204
00:11:53,733 --> 00:11:54,833
or even as an extra.

205
00:11:55,158 --> 00:11:56,408
$6 please

206
00:11:56,500 --> 00:11:57,200
in advance.

207
00:11:58,033 --> 00:11:58,500
Oh.

208
00:12:09,250 --> 00:12:10,350
Miss Esther Blodgett:

209
00:12:10,400 --> 00:12:12,066
Your application for extra work received.

210
00:12:12,133 --> 00:12:13,900
All extra talent is engaged by this studio,

211
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:16,000
as by all other major studios,

212
00:12:16,066 --> 00:12:18,100
through the Central Casting Corporation at.

213
00:12:19,166 --> 00:12:20,466
DO YOU UNDERSTAND FIGURES?

214
00:12:20,500 --> 00:12:21,566
Extras Registered with us:

215
00:12:21,600 --> 00:12:22,650
Women - 5,393

216
00:12:22,700 --> 00:12:23,733
Men - 5,517

217
00:12:23,800 --> 00:12:24,800
Children - 1,506

218
00:12:24,900 --> 00:12:26,166
Total 12,146

219
00:12:46,533 --> 00:12:47,833
I beg your pardon,

220
00:12:48,853 --> 00:12:50,658
I'd like to register for extra work.

221
00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:52,800
How long have you been in Hollywood?

222
00:12:53,633 --> 00:12:55,066
Well, it's about a month now.

223
00:12:55,866 --> 00:12:58,300
We haven't put anyone on our books for over two years.

224
00:13:00,866 --> 00:13:01,866
Come here,

225
00:13:05,033 --> 00:13:06,408
I'd like to show you something.

226
00:13:11,100 --> 00:13:14,966
- Try later.
- No can't be, try later.

227
00:13:15,300 --> 00:13:18,233
- Try later, thank you.
- Not now, try later.

228
00:13:18,966 --> 00:13:22,100
Every time you see one of those little lights flash it's somebody asking for a job.

229
00:13:23,100 --> 00:13:24,333
every time you hear them say: "Try later",

230
00:13:24,366 --> 00:13:25,933
it means there isn't any job.

231
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:31,033
Can't keep the girls at the switchboard long, they'd go crazy.

232
00:13:32,866 --> 00:13:35,300
Every one of those little lights thought it was going to be a star.

233
00:13:36,700 --> 00:13:38,066
Still wanna go in the movies?

234
00:13:43,242 --> 00:13:44,533
You know what your chances are?

235
00:13:45,825 --> 00:13:47,242
One in a hundred thousand.

236
00:13:55,166 --> 00:13:55,800
Maybe...

237
00:13:57,033 --> 00:13:58,033
...I'm that one.

238
00:14:10,766 --> 00:14:12,233
Any phone calls for me, Mr. Randall?

239
00:14:13,200 --> 00:14:13,800
oh, no.

240
00:14:15,400 --> 00:14:18,733
Jesse L. Lasky and Sammy Goldwyn must be writting letters instead.

241
00:14:21,283 --> 00:14:22,200
How was the luck today?

242
00:14:22,492 --> 00:14:23,408
There wasn't any.

243
00:14:24,366 --> 00:14:26,200
Maybe you don't go at in the right way

244
00:14:26,800 --> 00:14:30,000
Now take Danny McGuire here, he knows the ropes. Don't you Danny?

245
00:14:30,266 --> 00:14:32,133
Sure, I've had 'em around my neck for years.

246
00:14:37,166 --> 00:14:37,500
What?

247
00:14:37,900 --> 00:14:39,600
Oh, Miss Blodgett,

248
00:14:39,783 --> 00:14:41,325
Danny McGuire, our new tenant

249
00:14:41,950 --> 00:14:42,575
How do you do?

250
00:14:43,033 --> 00:14:44,283
Mr. McGuire is a...

251
00:14:44,700 --> 00:14:45,617
...big director.

252
00:14:48,950 --> 00:14:49,867
Oh.

253
00:14:50,366 --> 00:14:51,666
Are you really?

254
00:14:53,400 --> 00:14:56,233
Oh, could you possibly use me in a picture, Mr. McGuire?

255
00:14:56,766 --> 00:14:58,700
Of course I haven't had much experience but

256
00:14:58,933 --> 00:15:01,100
I don't think that really matters if you're willing.

257
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:02,033
- And I'm --

258
00:15:02,066 --> 00:15:02,900
Now listen, lady.

259
00:15:03,433 --> 00:15:06,900
In the first place I'm not a director, I'm an assistant director

260
00:15:07,033 --> 00:15:08,366
and the second place

261
00:15:08,700 --> 00:15:12,133
if I had any jobs to give away, I'd confirm one of them myself.

262
00:15:12,500 --> 00:15:16,000
and in the third place, you should have stayed back home in the first place!

263
00:15:21,133 --> 00:15:22,966
Oh, no! Look what you've done.

264
00:15:23,300 --> 00:15:24,733
Hey, wait a minute!

265
00:15:25,166 --> 00:15:26,933
Hey, don't be that way!

266
00:15:29,400 --> 00:15:30,300
Don't do that.

267
00:15:32,300 --> 00:15:34,333
Gosh, I didn't mean to get tough.

268
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:37,000
A guy thinks he's being kidded when

269
00:15:37,894 --> 00:15:40,033
somebody asking for a job and,

270
00:15:41,033 --> 00:15:42,700
he hasn't got one for himself.

271
00:15:46,117 --> 00:15:48,367
After all, I'm not a big enough shot to

272
00:15:48,566 --> 00:15:49,433
hurt your feelings.

273
00:15:49,733 --> 00:15:50,966
I'm sorry.

274
00:15:51,533 --> 00:15:53,658
It wasn't just that, oh, it was a lot of things.

275
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,933
I've been for a job everyday and never getting any nearer to it.

276
00:15:57,575 --> 00:15:59,367
I guess I was beginning to get a little scared.

277
00:16:00,300 --> 00:16:01,066
I know.

278
00:16:01,700 --> 00:16:03,533
Lady, do I know.

279
00:16:04,100 --> 00:16:06,533
Well, There's only one thing to do when you're feeling

280
00:16:06,700 --> 00:16:09,066
tired and sunk and down to your last nickle.

281
00:16:09,133 --> 00:16:10,566
Come on and I'll buy you a drink.

282
00:16:10,933 --> 00:16:12,900
Well its not as bad as down to the last nickle

283
00:16:13,100 --> 00:16:14,533
I've still got $11 left.

284
00:16:14,933 --> 00:16:16,233
$11 dollars!

285
00:16:16,700 --> 00:16:19,133
You're gonna buy me a drink! Come on!

286
00:16:25,966 --> 00:16:26,900
That's right George

287
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:28,633
there's nothing like a little rum to

288
00:16:28,666 --> 00:16:30,266
take away that milk flavor.

289
00:16:39,133 --> 00:16:41,066
I beg your pardon.

290
00:16:41,233 --> 00:16:42,200
Certainly.

291
00:16:50,800 --> 00:16:54,000
and when I sign my contract, the first thing
I'm gonna do is see that you direct every picture that I'm in.

292
00:16:54,300 --> 00:16:55,733
Thats my pal!

293
00:16:57,366 --> 00:16:59,166
Of course I'm going to be perfectly nice about it, but

294
00:16:59,600 --> 00:17:00,900
I'll just insist.

295
00:17:03,825 --> 00:17:05,408
Now that's the way to talk.

296
00:17:05,742 --> 00:17:07,492
Don't let 'em lick ya.

297
00:17:07,600 --> 00:17:08,533
They can't lick me,

298
00:17:08,733 --> 00:17:10,100
If they anything like that well,

299
00:17:12,500 --> 00:17:13,800
I just won't sign.

300
00:17:14,950 --> 00:17:17,462
Now that's right.
What have you got to lose?

301
00:17:18,308 --> 00:17:21,641
Another one of these and we'll open our own studio.

302
00:17:28,266 --> 00:17:28,966
Bill rendered,

303
00:17:29,233 --> 00:17:30,333
twenty four dollars.

304
00:17:30,866 --> 00:17:31,933
Past due.

305
00:17:32,366 --> 00:17:34,600
Remit without further delay.

306
00:17:35,800 --> 00:17:36,566
Me.
<u>(The Management)</u>

307
00:17:46,400 --> 00:17:47,033
Poor kid.

308
00:17:47,133 --> 00:17:50,200
If she can't pay the bills she gets

309
00:17:51,100 --> 00:17:51,800
No.

310
00:18:09,500 --> 00:18:13,466
Fifth race, fifth race, I can't... I can't believe it...

311
00:18:17,558 --> 00:18:19,812
HOLLYWOOD BOWL

312
00:18:32,165 --> 00:18:34,162
Hey! The program is gonna be swell tonight.

313
00:18:34,433 --> 00:18:36,300
Now you take this fella Beethoven:

314
00:18:36,400 --> 00:18:37,933
I'm a pushover for him.

315
00:18:38,200 --> 00:18:39,200
And Chopin -

316
00:18:39,600 --> 00:18:41,133
well, he's not so dusty, either.

317
00:18:41,400 --> 00:18:43,533
But I kinda wish that once in awhile that

318
00:18:43,700 --> 00:18:46,166
play something you could sorta go out whistling.

319
00:18:46,400 --> 00:18:49,466
You know, like "Blood on the saddle, blood on..."

320
00:18:51,333 --> 00:18:52,966
well now there's a tune

321
00:18:53,500 --> 00:18:56,033
Hey, why don't you throw your hat in the air or something. Can't you?

322
00:18:56,200 --> 00:18:58,566
but this is a celebration, my job starts tomorrow

323
00:18:58,700 --> 00:19:00,900
I know it does, and I think it's swell, Danny.

324
00:19:01,733 --> 00:19:03,533
Gee, I wish you were in on it too.

325
00:19:04,133 --> 00:19:06,600
But oh no, it would have to be a war picture.

326
00:19:06,933 --> 00:19:10,975
A big novelty number. The war picture without any beautiful women at the front.

327
00:19:11,700 --> 00:19:13,300
Oh well, something'll happen soon.

328
00:19:13,433 --> 00:19:14,000
Maybe.

329
00:19:15,966 --> 00:19:17,500
why don't you go home kid?

330
00:19:18,600 --> 00:19:20,333
Oh Danny, I can't do that.

331
00:19:20,533 --> 00:19:22,500
I came here and I've got to stay.

332
00:19:23,233 --> 00:19:25,033
Well now, if it's on account of money I can...

333
00:19:25,200 --> 00:19:26,866
Thank you but you given enough already

334
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,900
Anyhow this is no time to be worrying, this is a party!

335
00:19:32,133 --> 00:19:33,633
Look at all the people.

336
00:19:33,833 --> 00:19:35,900
Everybody in the world,

337
00:19:37,500 --> 00:19:38,066
Look

338
00:19:40,166 --> 00:19:41,700
That's Norman Maine

339
00:19:48,200 --> 00:19:51,633
seems to have had that one extra cocktail

340
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:01,700
Oh, Thank you.

341
00:20:04,300 --> 00:20:07,500
Sit down you dope that's for the orchestra leader.

342
00:20:14,400 --> 00:20:16,700
Hello...
Hello, Norman.

343
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,233
Oh, Mr. Maine, Mr. Maine,

344
00:20:20,633 --> 00:20:22,300
Put your arm around Miss Regis.

345
00:20:22,433 --> 00:20:25,333
Arlow, this is the Hollywood Bowl.

346
00:20:26,066 --> 00:20:27,200
oh, afraid of crowds?

347
00:20:27,400 --> 00:20:29,033
Go ahead, go on, Get outta here!

348
00:20:29,833 --> 00:20:32,500
What's the matter? You getting too big to bother with photographers?

349
00:20:32,666 --> 00:20:34,733
don't want any pictures taken now.

350
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:38,233
Oh is that so? Well supposing I take it anyway

351
00:20:38,700 --> 00:20:41,200
I'll shove that Brownie Number 2 of yours down your throat.

352
00:20:41,400 --> 00:20:42,700
Thank you! Mr. Maine!

353
00:20:42,866 --> 00:20:44,000
No! Stop it!

354
00:20:45,333 --> 00:20:45,833
No!

355
00:20:46,600 --> 00:20:47,266
Stop it!

356
00:20:47,500 --> 00:20:48,700
No, Stop!

357
00:20:49,300 --> 00:20:50,266
Come on!

358
00:20:50,966 --> 00:20:51,900
Take it easy, Mr. Maine!

359
00:20:52,200 --> 00:20:52,866
Hey, take your hands off.

360
00:20:52,966 --> 00:20:53,900
Calm down, please.

361
00:20:55,500 --> 00:20:58,766
Norman! come back and sit down everybody's laughing at you.

362
00:21:05,400 --> 00:21:06,033
Alright, already...

363
00:21:06,100 --> 00:21:06,866
Hi, Norman.

364
00:21:09,333 --> 00:21:10,633
Is he always like that?

365
00:21:11,275 --> 00:21:13,633
Well, I suppose he has to sleep sometime.

366
00:21:13,733 --> 00:21:16,166
Oh and he is so wonderful on the screen.

367
00:21:33,600 --> 00:21:35,700
...and thanks again, dear Granny, for the money -

368
00:21:35,800 --> 00:21:37,933
but you mustn't keep sending it.

369
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,033
Everything is going beautifully now.

370
00:21:40,100 --> 00:21:42,333
In fact, Daniel McGuire, the director,

371
00:21:42,400 --> 00:21:45,500
says I'm certain to get a job very soon.

372
00:21:48,800 --> 00:21:49,300
Come in.

373
00:21:51,433 --> 00:21:52,200
Hello, Danny.

374
00:21:53,400 --> 00:21:54,266
What's the matter?

375
00:21:54,866 --> 00:21:57,233
Belive it or not, I've got a job for you.

376
00:21:57,300 --> 00:21:58,300
Ah!

377
00:21:58,400 --> 00:22:01,200
Danny, that's wonderful! When do I go to the studio?

378
00:22:02,066 --> 00:22:04,200
Well, you don't exactly know there's a studio.

379
00:22:04,300 --> 00:22:05,433
Oh! It's on location.

380
00:22:05,600 --> 00:22:07,400
No, it's not exactly on location.

381
00:22:07,533 --> 00:22:11,000
Of course I haven't any makeup. Will you tell me what to get and sort of help me put it on?

382
00:22:11,133 --> 00:22:13,100
Well, you don't exactly need any makeup,

383
00:22:13,200 --> 00:22:15,633
you see it's not really a picture job

384
00:22:15,700 --> 00:22:17,866
it's, it's well it's being a waitress.

385
00:22:20,800 --> 00:22:21,366
Oh.

386
00:22:22,883 --> 00:22:25,925
Well it's kind of a picture job if you look at it right.

387
00:22:27,100 --> 00:22:28,533
but you said it was a waitress.

388
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:30,733
Well, it's waitressing for Casey Burt.

389
00:22:30,733 --> 00:22:34,033
He's a big director over at our studio, he's giving a party tonight

390
00:22:34,100 --> 00:22:36,833
to kind of celebrate on account of finishing the picture

391
00:22:37,258 --> 00:22:40,675
and he wanted me to get him an extra waitress and it's 5 dollars.

392
00:22:41,300 --> 00:22:43,105
and I thought of you right away, Esther.

393
00:22:43,400 --> 00:22:45,400
That was awfully sweet of you Danny.

394
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:46,633
Well,

395
00:22:47,033 --> 00:22:49,766
They're gonna be a lot of big people at Burke's house

396
00:22:49,800 --> 00:22:51,900
I'll bet you there's any number of big directors

397
00:22:52,266 --> 00:22:54,200
and if you're there maybe they'll notice you.

398
00:22:56,966 --> 00:22:58,166
I could make them notice me.

399
00:22:58,300 --> 00:23:00,600
Sure you could Ester! It's your chance!

400
00:23:02,600 --> 00:23:03,400
My chance.

401
00:23:04,600 --> 00:23:06,466
Alright Danny I'll do it, oh

402
00:23:06,900 --> 00:23:09,100
but I can't, I haven't got the right things to wear.

403
00:23:09,333 --> 00:23:10,933
Oh! Oh! Here!

404
00:23:14,533 --> 00:23:18,000
You don't think the wardrobe department is right next to my office for nothing, do you?

405
00:23:18,633 --> 00:23:20,675
Haha a perfect fit!

406
00:23:36,300 --> 00:23:37,900
Did you get to the preview last night?

407
00:23:38,066 --> 00:23:38,733
I did.

408
00:23:41,925 --> 00:23:45,008
Vould you like a little hors d'oeuvre?

409
00:23:45,600 --> 00:23:47,533
They are very nice.

410
00:23:48,300 --> 00:23:48,900
Well thanks.

411
00:23:49,883 --> 00:23:51,050
What did you think of the picture?

412
00:23:51,800 --> 00:23:53,300
You should have saved it for Thanksgiving.

413
00:23:53,500 --> 00:23:54,033
What a turkey!

414
00:24:01,666 --> 00:24:04,833
Will you have some hors d'oeuvres? You do like hors d'oeuvres don't you?

415
00:24:04,966 --> 00:24:08,866
I don't think there's anything so enjoyable as hors d'oeuvres before supper

416
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:11,400
And these are really delightful.

417
00:24:22,675 --> 00:24:25,592
and at the finish, the kid turns around and sings a lullaby to its mother.

418
00:24:25,900 --> 00:24:27,500
Pardon me, big boy

419
00:24:28,200 --> 00:24:31,066
but, would you like a little, hors d'oeuvres?

420
00:24:31,800 --> 00:24:33,466
They say they're the best.

421
00:24:34,333 --> 00:24:35,300
In town.

422
00:24:35,433 --> 00:24:36,266
Don't tell me.

423
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:37,666
I know.

424
00:24:37,800 --> 00:24:38,500
Mae West.

425
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:42,966
That's a great twist! But where are you gonna find a two month old baby that can sing?

426
00:24:46,200 --> 00:24:46,933
Hello, Oliver!

427
00:24:47,066 --> 00:24:47,800
Oh, hello Casey!

428
00:24:48,592 --> 00:24:50,550
Wanna fire me now or wait till you see the picture?

429
00:24:51,166 --> 00:24:52,666
I'm not a director anymore I'm a...

430
00:24:53,133 --> 00:24:53,787
male nurse!

431
00:24:54,550 --> 00:24:55,800
What's the matter with the picture?

432
00:24:55,933 --> 00:24:57,366
A guy by the name of Norman Main

433
00:24:58,700 --> 00:25:00,966
His work is beginning to interfere with his drinking.

434
00:25:02,833 --> 00:25:03,300
Oliver,

435
00:25:03,666 --> 00:25:06,700
don't tell me I'm to direct his next picture, too. -mhm

436
00:25:08,033 --> 00:25:09,666
You were my favorite producer.

437
00:25:10,050 --> 00:25:12,133
Now wait a minute, you just go right on with your directing.

438
00:25:12,758 --> 00:25:14,133
I'll take care of these stars.

439
00:25:14,342 --> 00:25:15,425
I know how to handle them.

440
00:25:15,800 --> 00:25:18,733
I had a serious talk with Norman after that
Hollywood Bowl occurrence.

441
00:25:19,100 --> 00:25:21,100
And you not have to worry any more about his behavior.

442
00:25:21,433 --> 00:25:22,366
Excuse me, Mister Niles.

443
00:25:23,100 --> 00:25:25,500
Mr Libby, of your publicity department is on the telephone.

444
00:25:25,833 --> 00:25:27,233
He says it's most important Sir.

445
00:25:27,833 --> 00:25:28,800
It's about Mr. Maine.

446
00:25:29,233 --> 00:25:29,766
Thank you.

447
00:25:32,700 --> 00:25:34,800
Oh, it's probably just some little thing.

448
00:25:35,092 --> 00:25:36,175
Of course, Oliver!

449
00:25:36,600 --> 00:25:37,600
I´ll turn on the radio and see

450
00:25:37,700 --> 00:25:39,766
If he's called out the National Guard yet.

451
00:25:41,633 --> 00:25:42,933
Hello Libby, what´s the good word?

452
00:25:43,700 --> 00:25:47,266
Mister Norman Maine, America's prince charming,

453
00:25:47,900 --> 00:25:49,800
Was apprehended driving an ambulance

454
00:25:49,966 --> 00:25:51,233
Down Wilshire Blvd!

455
00:25:51,633 --> 00:25:53,675
with a siren going full blast

456
00:25:54,050 --> 00:25:57,342
He explained he was a tree surgeon on a maternity case.

457
00:25:57,833 --> 00:25:59,100
Well... er, will it be in the papers?

458
00:25:59,533 --> 00:26:01,066
No it won't be in the papers.

459
00:26:01,633 --> 00:26:04,092
But that's a nice expensive hobby of yours.

460
00:26:04,300 --> 00:26:07,833
Keeping Mr. Maine's informal entertainment out of the public press.

461
00:26:09,533 --> 00:26:11,100
Oh, that´s fine work Libby

462
00:26:11,433 --> 00:26:13,066
Try and see that no one gets to Norman.

463
00:26:13,433 --> 00:26:15,466
He's probably home sleeping it off.

464
00:26:16,983 --> 00:26:19,775
Because you forgot those dopes at the studio for one night

465
00:26:20,300 --> 00:26:21,500
Business, business all the time.

466
00:26:21,650 --> 00:26:23,275
I dont know what´s gonna become of you

467
00:26:25,666 --> 00:26:26,500
Norman!

468
00:26:27,442 --> 00:26:28,900
Why didn't you call for me?

469
00:26:29,633 --> 00:26:31,366
Oh my darling why didn't I call for you?

470
00:26:32,400 --> 00:26:34,933
In case you've forgotten, I was supposed to come here with you.

471
00:26:35,133 --> 00:26:37,266
Oh that old hat's alright, I got here without any trouble.

472
00:26:37,900 --> 00:26:39,500
The only reason I don't slap your face --

473
00:26:39,733 --> 00:26:41,600
Yes, yes, darling. I- I know.

474
00:26:42,358 --> 00:26:43,150
Hello John!

475
00:26:43,433 --> 00:26:44,200
Oh. Hello, Norman!

476
00:26:44,200 --> 00:26:46,300
What's the matter with Oliver?
he looks as if he got bad news.

477
00:26:48,400 --> 00:26:48,733
Hello!

478
00:26:48,733 --> 00:26:49,333
Hello Larry.

479
00:26:51,400 --> 00:26:52,300
What's the matter, old boy?

480
00:26:53,266 --> 00:26:54,100
Maybe I'm wrong.

481
00:26:54,200 --> 00:26:55,633
I guess I've been drinking too much lately.

482
00:26:55,766 --> 00:26:57,566
Oh you ought to cut that down, it's bad stuff

483
00:26:58,200 --> 00:26:59,033
Scotch and soda

484
00:27:05,184 --> 00:27:06,345
Come on, come on, come on.

485
00:27:07,400 --> 00:27:08,933
The word you know, is pronounced 'when'.

486
00:27:10,067 --> 00:27:11,400
Bad dialog Oliver.

487
00:27:12,108 --> 00:27:13,108
Would rather not watch this

488
00:27:14,666 --> 00:27:15,433
You know best

489
00:27:16,442 --> 00:27:17,275
Soda!

490
00:27:19,266 --> 00:27:20,433
Thank you!

491
00:27:21,666 --> 00:27:22,133
Uh.

492
00:27:25,525 --> 00:27:26,400
Go ahead and say it.

493
00:27:26,733 --> 00:27:27,666
Got it coming to me.

494
00:27:28,333 --> 00:27:29,800
Don't make it tougher on me Norman.

495
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:31,433
I don't want to stand here and preach,

496
00:27:31,500 --> 00:27:34,466
but take a look at my side of it, I'm trying to make pictures with you

497
00:27:34,666 --> 00:27:35,100
I know, I know.

498
00:27:35,817 --> 00:27:38,025
Costs are going up and the grosses are going down.

499
00:27:38,033 --> 00:27:38,933
No, it isn't that.

500
00:27:39,233 --> 00:27:40,433
I made lots of money with you.

501
00:27:40,733 --> 00:27:41,866
And I can afford to take a loss,

502
00:27:42,200 --> 00:27:44,100
but I hate to see you going the way of so many others.

503
00:27:44,233 --> 00:27:46,833
Why don't you get Lloyds to insure you against me?

504
00:27:47,100 --> 00:27:49,700
You can't get insurance against a man forgetting who he is.

505
00:27:50,358 --> 00:27:54,400
You're a great star Norman, but there's nobody so big they can afford to have people refuse to work with him.

506
00:27:54,692 --> 00:27:56,400
Who doesn't wanna work with me? You--

507
00:27:56,433 --> 00:27:56,900
Quiet.

508
00:27:57,200 --> 00:27:58,666
Listen, I know plenty people who do.

509
00:27:58,800 --> 00:27:59,666
Yes and so do I

510
00:27:59,800 --> 00:28:02,600
but your real friends can't stand seeing you start to fall apart.

511
00:28:03,150 --> 00:28:03,900
What do you mean by that?

512
00:28:04,400 --> 00:28:05,866
First signs are always the same

513
00:28:06,033 --> 00:28:07,733
not being able to remember your lines.

514
00:28:08,192 --> 00:28:09,942
Camera man struggling to cover your hangovers

515
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:11,600
And all because you have to have a good time...

516
00:28:11,733 --> 00:28:12,900
...every day and every night.

517
00:28:13,200 --> 00:28:14,266
Listen I've warned you for a long time.

518
00:28:14,400 --> 00:28:15,700
I know, Oliver you're a swell guy,

519
00:28:15,733 --> 00:28:17,900
you won't lose any money on me.
