01:26:53,000 --> 01:26:55,000
and perhaps, if you want to consider it for later on,

1580
01:26:55,100 --> 01:26:57,400
Well, I tell ya Oliver, you better not count on me.

1581
01:26:58,390 --> 01:27:03,410
See, I've got several pictures lined up after this, and then they're talking to me about England

1582
01:27:03,900 --> 01:27:06,900
You know, they're doing some very interesting things over there

1583
01:27:06,900 --> 01:27:08,200
Hey

1584
01:27:09,200 --> 01:27:11,500
What is it? Cuddle speak right out. We all love you.

1585
01:27:11,800 --> 01:27:13,500
The dinner.
-Oh!

1586
01:27:14,100 --> 01:27:18,200
We dine at five thirty here, makes the nights longer.

1587
01:27:18,800 --> 01:27:22,800
Now, goodbye Norman. I'm glad to see you're getting along so well.

1588
01:27:22,900 --> 01:27:24,100
I'll be out in no time.

1589
01:27:24,700 --> 01:27:30,100
I'll have to introduce myself all over to a lot of people, won't know when I'm not drinking.

1590
01:27:30,400 --> 01:27:31,100
Goodbye.
-Goodbye.

1591
01:27:31,200 --> 01:27:32,700
Goodbye Norman.
-Thanks for dropping in.

1592
01:27:37,100 --> 01:27:41,200
Well, cuddles... alone at last.

1593
01:27:46,700 --> 01:27:49,700
Christmas Week
Racing
Santa Anita Park

1594
01:28:28,500 --> 01:28:29,000
Oh, hello.

1595
01:28:29,000 --> 01:28:29,400
-Hello
-Hello

1596
01:28:30,930 --> 01:28:32,160
Hello, Norman.

1597
01:28:35,260 --> 01:28:36,610
I see.

1598
01:28:38,840 --> 01:28:41,690
How I hate to run into these has-beens.

1599
01:28:41,800 --> 01:28:42,700
Give me the creeps.

1600
01:28:42,760 --> 01:28:43,910
Me too.

1601
01:28:44,700 --> 01:28:46,500
He was good while he had it.

1602
01:28:46,880 --> 01:28:48,460
And he had it quite a while.

1603
01:28:49,400 --> 01:28:51,500
-Hello
-Hello Mr. Maine

1604
01:28:51,600 --> 01:28:54,300
-I haven't seen you in a long time.
-Ah, I've been resting

1605
01:28:55,130 --> 01:28:56,420
Ginger ale, please.

1606
01:28:56,730 --> 01:28:58,220
Ginger ale and what?

1607
01:28:58,700 --> 01:29:01,300
Ginger ale and ginger ale.

1608
01:29:01,500 --> 01:29:02,100
New leaf?

1609
01:29:02,590 --> 01:29:04,190
A whole new book.

1610
01:29:05,750 --> 01:29:06,810
Thank you.

1611
01:29:09,380 --> 01:29:11,150
Scotch straight.

1612
01:29:11,600 --> 01:29:12,800
Hello Libby

1613
01:29:13,750 --> 01:29:16,090
Why it's Mr. America of yesteryear.

1614
01:29:16,820 --> 01:29:20,150
Do they let you wander about now without a keeping?

1615
01:29:23,700 --> 01:29:26,020
I didn't expect to find you an Santa Anita.

1616
01:29:26,630 --> 01:29:29,410
What do they do with the actors when you're away?

1617
01:29:29,500 --> 01:29:32,500
Oh, they cut them into slices and fry them with eggs.

1618
01:29:38,100 --> 01:29:40,590
Suppose you'll be here all the time

1619
01:29:40,800 --> 01:29:45,500
Now that you've retired from the hurly-burly of the silver screen.

1620
01:29:45,800 --> 01:29:52,400
Well, I'm living down in Malibu now. Pretty lonesome with Esther away working all day.

1621
01:29:53,000 --> 01:29:55,700
I wouldn't squawk about that if I were you.

1622
01:29:56,780 --> 01:29:59,290
It's nice having somebody in the family making a living.

1623
01:29:59,420 --> 01:30:00,610
Oh Well, I'm gonna tell you.

1624
01:30:02,200 --> 01:30:03,700
I don't want to forget that we're friends.

1625
01:30:03,800 --> 01:30:06,200
Friends my eye. Say listen

1626
01:30:06,230 --> 01:30:10,760
I got you out of your jams because I had to, it was my job not because I was your friend.

1627
01:30:11,000 --> 01:30:14,500
I don't like you and I never have liked you. Nothing made me happier

1628
01:30:14,670 --> 01:30:20,330
than to see all those cute little pranks of yours has finally catch up with you and land you on your celebrated face!

1629
01:30:20,830 --> 01:30:25,420
Pretty work, Libby. Always wait till they're down, then kick them.

1630
01:30:25,700 --> 01:30:29,400
I don't feel sorry for you. You've fixed yourself nice and comfortable.

1631
01:30:29,700 --> 01:30:34,600
You can live off your wife now. She'll buy you drinks and put up with you even though nobody else will.

1632
01:30:45,100 --> 01:30:46,800
Wait a minute, Wait a minute...

1633
01:30:46,800 --> 01:30:48,220
Come on outside for your right, pal!

1634
01:30:48,300 --> 01:30:49,400
I am Norman Maine!

1635
01:30:49,410 --> 01:30:52,220
Oh, Don't bother to throw him out.

1636
01:30:52,300 --> 01:30:54,200
-He's harmless.
-Alright, Mr. Libby if you say so.

1637
01:30:54,300 --> 01:30:57,800
We should let him go, What can he do?
He can't fight any better that he can act.

1638
01:30:57,940 --> 01:31:00,100
Norman Maine...

1639
01:31:17,200 --> 01:31:23,500
Give me Scotch...double.
Leave the bottle here.

1640
01:31:30,220 --> 01:31:32,790
Vicki, you'll be ill.
Why don't you try to get some sleep?

1641
01:31:33,230 --> 01:31:36,210
He's been gone 4 days, 4 days and not a word.

1642
01:31:41,400 --> 01:31:43,130
Oliver I can't.

1643
01:31:46,460 --> 01:31:51,930
Hello? No, this is Oliver Niles speaking. What? Where?

1644
01:31:53,520 --> 01:31:54,400
Thank you.

1645
01:31:54,700 --> 01:31:55,100
What is it?

1646
01:31:55,130 --> 01:31:56,120
Nothing, nothing.

1647
01:31:56,140 --> 01:31:57,310
Oliver tell me.

1648
01:31:57,400 --> 01:32:01,600
He's in the night court. He's been arrested on a drunk charge and he's alright, isn't hurt and I'm going right now to get him out.

1649
01:32:01,700 --> 01:32:02,300
I'm going with you.

1650
01:32:02,300 --> 01:32:04,400
Vicki, is isn't many place for you and if it gets in the paper-

1651
01:32:04,400 --> 01:32:07,880
What I care about the paper? I'm going with you!

1652
01:32:14,000 --> 01:32:17,200
Precinct 30, Municipal Court
County Los Angeles.

1653
01:32:17,250 --> 01:32:19,140
The honorable judge Jeff Harris presiding.

1654
01:32:19,200 --> 01:32:20,500
Be seated, Please.

1655
01:32:21,620 --> 01:32:22,960
Oliver, did you do anything?

1656
01:32:23,120 --> 01:32:24,660
The judge wouldn't even see me.

1657
01:32:24,830 --> 01:32:25,960
Ready, Your honor.

1658
01:32:31,460 --> 01:32:34,750
I want to advise you that you are entitled
to be represented by counsel,

1659
01:32:35,100 --> 01:32:37,700
to be confronted by the witnesses that may testified against you,

1660
01:32:37,800 --> 01:32:40,300
To compel witnesses to attend on your behalf

1661
01:32:40,400 --> 01:32:43,400
to the public and a speedy trial by the court, by a jury

1662
01:32:43,500 --> 01:32:46,440
and the right to place a bail.
Call the first five.

1663
01:32:46,600 --> 01:32:51,100
Gregory, Railes, Maine, Rodríguez, Johnson
Come on, Let's go!

1664
01:32:52,290 --> 01:32:53,930
Go ahead, Move Along.

1665
01:32:57,300 --> 01:33:00,300
-William Gregory
-Yes

1666
01:33:01,690 --> 01:33:04,050
Plain drunk, picked up in fifth and towne

1667
01:33:04,440 --> 01:33:07,410
Sleeping in the gutter, 14 similar offenses in the past 6 months.

1668
01:33:07,670 --> 01:33:09,580
Still at it, Gregory? How do you plead?

1669
01:33:09,830 --> 01:33:11,500
I don't feel so good.

1670
01:33:11,950 --> 01:33:14,590
I didn't ask how you feel, I ask you how you plead?!

1671
01:33:14,700 --> 01:33:15,800
Guilty, I guess.

1672
01:33:16,000 --> 01:33:17,800
When did you get out the last time?

1673
01:33:18,400 --> 01:33:21,100
Day before christmas.

1674
01:33:21,170 --> 01:33:25,690
I'm sorry. You'll have to miss New Years. You should be out in time for Washington's birthday. Sixty days.

1675
01:33:28,200 --> 01:33:30,020
Milton Railes

1676
01:33:33,560 --> 01:33:35,100
Plain drunk,

1677
01:33:35,200 --> 01:33:39,400
Picked up im Brooklyn avenue, giving treatment at residence hospital, then removed to jail.

1678
01:33:39,410 --> 01:33:41,030
How old are you, Railes?

1679
01:33:41,700 --> 01:33:43,200
Uh, seventeen, sir.

1680
01:33:43,290 --> 01:33:46,120
Did you get a good look at those men in the cell with you last night?

1681
01:33:47,240 --> 01:33:48,080
Yes, sir.

1682
01:33:48,480 --> 01:33:50,600
Have you taken a good look at yourself this morning?

1683
01:33:51,360 --> 01:33:52,480
Well, no sir.

1684
01:33:52,720 --> 01:33:55,700
Well, I suggest that you do.
Five dollars and two days.

1685
01:33:57,360 --> 01:33:58,830
Sentence suspended.

1686
01:33:59,010 --> 01:34:00,440
Oh, judge! I...

1687
01:34:00,960 --> 01:34:02,430
Alfred Henckle!

1688
01:34:02,740 --> 01:34:05,200
Commonly Known as Norman Maine.

1689
01:34:05,200 --> 01:34:09,600
Drinking disorderly, Wreck a car into a tree in Sunset and Coronado.

1690
01:34:09,920 --> 01:34:14,230
Evidently been drinking for days, Resisted arrest and injured one of the arresting officers.

1691
01:34:14,810 --> 01:34:16,470
How do you plead?

1692
01:34:16,730 --> 01:34:17,540
Guilty.

1693
01:34:18,260 --> 01:34:20,760
You're Norman Maine, the actor, aren't you?

1694
01:34:22,870 --> 01:34:25,120
You've come pretty low, haven't you?

1695
01:34:25,360 --> 01:34:28,880
There isn't a man here who's had the advantages you've had. Look what you've done with them.

1696
01:34:28,930 --> 01:34:35,370
You're nothing but an irresponsible drunk,
Driving above the streets with the power to inflict death or injury to innocent people.

1697
01:34:36,350 --> 01:34:38,590
We better deny you that power for a while.

1698
01:34:38,700 --> 01:34:40,600
Ninety days in the city jail.

1699
01:34:41,140 --> 01:34:42,350
Please wait.

1700
01:34:50,700 --> 01:34:51,700
I'm his wife.

1701
01:34:52,100 --> 01:34:54,090
Yes, I recognized you Ms. Lester.

1702
01:34:57,500 --> 01:35:02,000
Please, Judge. I promise you, this won't happen again.

1703
01:35:02,800 --> 01:35:08,200
I'll be responsible for him... if you just won't send him there.

1704
01:35:09,150 --> 01:35:13,880
Do you realize that this man, when drunk, is obviously a menace to public safety?

1705
01:35:15,860 --> 01:35:17,930
and you realized too Ms. Lester,

1706
01:35:18,130 --> 01:35:21,560
the responsibility you'll be assuming to this court...

1707
01:35:22,260 --> 01:35:23,610
I do.

1708
01:35:27,620 --> 01:35:30,940
Sentence suspended.
Prisioner remanded to custody of wife.

1709
01:35:33,550 --> 01:35:36,090
You can meet him at the jail entrance madam.

1710
01:35:36,680 --> 01:35:38,830
José Rodriguez

1711
01:35:40,450 --> 01:35:43,720
Found drunk, picked up first of maine.
Second offense.

1712
01:35:44,120 --> 01:35:49,420
-How do you plead?
-I think I'm guilty, your honor
-Sixty days

1713
01:36:04,500 --> 01:36:05,800
I'm...

1714
01:36:05,810 --> 01:36:08,660
-I'm so tired.
-Hold it, Mr. Maine, a picture.

1715
01:36:08,660 --> 01:36:11,950
-Oh no, Please. -What about a picture to the press?
-Oh no, Please.

1716
01:36:12,700 --> 01:36:23,900
<i>Norman Maine Released to custody
of Vicki Lester after drunk Conviction!
Night court Drama as star Pleads for husband's freedom.</i>

1717
01:36:27,320 --> 01:36:30,160
He's still asleep. He's been asleep all day.

1718
01:36:30,500 --> 01:36:32,420
That's the best thing for him.

1719
01:36:32,650 --> 01:36:35,540
Is awful to see this happen to someone you love

1720
01:36:35,900 --> 01:36:39,400
and know in your heart that can't get any better.

1721
01:36:39,600 --> 01:36:43,200
All I know that, All I can do now is stay
with him and try to help him.

1722
01:36:43,200 --> 01:36:47,800
So am I.
...take care of him.

1723
01:36:47,830 --> 01:36:49,890
You'll very fond of him aren't you Oliver.

1724
01:36:50,300 --> 01:36:52,100
I'm very fond of both.

1725
01:36:53,870 --> 01:36:56,710
Then I know you'll understand what I have to tell you.

1726
01:36:57,170 --> 01:37:02,950
After what happen last night, I think you already know what it is.
I can't do any more pictures.

1727
01:37:03,000 --> 01:37:05,400
I'm going away for good with Norman.

1728
01:37:05,500 --> 01:37:10,600
You can't do that Vicki, You're in the very peak of your success and you work so hard to achieve.

1729
01:37:10,700 --> 01:37:20,600
This is for Norman, I thought it all out

1730
01:37:21,100 --> 01:37:23,000
I know it's too late to think about that now.

1731
01:37:23,100 --> 01:37:23,600
But...

1732
01:37:24,580 --> 01:37:28,140
May not too be late to go away with him and star over somewhere.

1733
01:37:28,250 --> 01:37:30,060
It's your life your giving up Vicki.

1734
01:37:32,070 --> 01:37:35,140
So I can try to get norman back his.

1735
01:37:37,150 --> 01:37:40,320
Can you honestly tell me I'm wrong to do it.

1736
01:37:42,540 --> 01:37:46,760
No Vicki, I can't honestly tell you that.

1737
01:37:49,700 --> 01:37:54,500
Then there'll be no more Vicki Lester.

1738
01:37:58,230 --> 01:38:00,020
Come on, walk to the door with me.

1739
01:38:13,870 --> 01:38:17,390
Goodbye, Vicki Lester. You were a grand girl.

1740
01:38:18,400 --> 01:38:20,500
Good luck, Mrs. Norman Maine.

1741
01:39:37,700 --> 01:39:39,100
Darling!

1742
01:39:41,700 --> 01:39:44,400
Maine coming in to apologize again.

1743
01:39:44,400 --> 01:39:47,300
Stop, I'm sorry Dear, but

1744
01:39:48,100 --> 01:39:49,700
It isn't you

1745
01:39:50,800 --> 01:39:52,900
What other troubles have you got?

1746
01:39:53,100 --> 01:39:54,600
None.

1747
01:39:55,500 --> 01:39:58,700
I was just playing a scene with myself.

1748
01:39:59,000 --> 01:40:04,300
Now look, I'm just coming out of the jitters and your just going into them.

1749
01:40:05,500 --> 01:40:07,500
This is swell housefold.

1750
01:40:07,600 --> 01:40:08,900
Isn't it?

1751
01:40:08,990 --> 01:40:14,360
Tell you what we'll do. I promise to brace up,
you go on the wagon.

1752
01:40:15,120 --> 01:40:18,140
I cannot have been drinking too mch.

1753
01:40:19,300 --> 01:40:22,900
What I'm gonna do, I'm going to be an athlete.

1754
01:40:23,200 --> 01:40:25,900
You mean with great big muscles and everything.

1755
01:40:25,990 --> 01:40:27,990
Well, roughly speaking.

1756
01:40:28,400 --> 01:40:31,500
Gonna join the YMCA?

1757
01:40:31,500 --> 01:40:35,200
No, cost too much. Now I'm going waiting out on the front yard.

1758
01:40:36,290 --> 01:40:37,760
-Now
-Sure!

1759
01:40:38,330 --> 01:40:40,010
Do you like me to go with you?

1760
01:40:40,910 --> 01:40:42,360
Sure, if you like to.

1761
01:40:43,170 --> 01:40:48,290
Norman, I don't think I will.
It might spoil this beatiful natural waves.

1762
01:40:49,100 --> 01:40:50,400
Yeah, I get that's right.

1763
01:40:50,600 --> 01:40:53,600
Darling, look.
Can I have a hot...

1764
01:40:54,700 --> 01:40:57,800
I mean some hot soup for me, when I come back?

1765
01:40:57,900 --> 01:40:59,400
A hot soup.

1766
01:40:59,400 --> 01:41:01,400
and I make one some of those nice sandwiches.

1767
01:41:01,760 --> 01:41:04,240
Norman, do you have to?

1768
01:41:13,700 --> 01:41:18,900
-Come on now.
-Don't stay out too long

1769
01:41:25,030 --> 01:41:26,530
Hey!

1770
01:41:28,970 --> 01:41:32,530
Do you mind if I take just... one more look?

1771
01:42:28,020 --> 01:42:33,700
<i>Norman Maine's body found of Malibu
Ex-Star perishes in tragic accident!</i>

1772
01:42:38,000 --> 01:42:43,800
First drink of water he had in twenty years.
Then, he had to get it by accident.

1773
01:42:43,930 --> 01:42:45,750
(Laughing)

1774
01:42:46,900 --> 01:42:52,600
but how do you write congratulations to the Pacific Ocean?

1775
01:42:52,680 --> 01:42:55,400
(Laughing)

1776
01:42:59,000 --> 01:42:59,900
There he is now!

1777
01:43:00,300 --> 01:43:02,220
Oh! You can't see a thing.

1778
01:43:04,570 --> 01:43:06,330
Hello Vicki!

1779
01:43:22,500 --> 01:43:25,700
-Get away, can't you?
-Come on, Vicki! Let's see your face.

1780
01:43:25,700 --> 01:43:28,700
Vicki, will you sign my book for me?
Write "This is Norman Maine".

1781
01:43:29,000 --> 01:43:31,000
Don't you care, Vicki. You'll get over it.

1782
01:43:33,300 --> 01:43:35,100
Stand back, can't you?

1783
01:43:36,320 --> 01:43:38,960
Don't you cry, dearie! He wasn't so much!

1784
01:44:04,420 --> 01:44:10,160
Do you mind if I take just... one more look?

1785
01:44:31,830 --> 01:44:34,130
Here are the paychecks for the servants, Graves.

1786
01:44:34,160 --> 01:44:36,530
You'll find a very nice bonus in each one.

1787
01:44:36,600 --> 01:44:39,700
Miss Nasta asked me to thank you for your kindness and service.

1788
01:44:39,800 --> 01:44:43,200
If there is anything i can do for the little lady, i should be glad to do it.

1789
01:44:43,300 --> 01:44:46,500
She would appreciate your attending to
the closing of the Beverly Hills house.

1790
01:44:46,710 --> 01:44:48,530
Be careful with that!

1791
01:44:48,900 --> 01:44:51,100
Put it down, I say!

1792
01:44:53,000 --> 01:44:54,500
Well? Where is she?

1793
01:44:54,500 --> 01:44:56,300
I-In the bedroom, who are you?

1794
01:44:56,400 --> 01:44:59,400
Oh, I'm her grandmother. Get out of the way!

1795
01:45:01,700 --> 01:45:02,900
Hey

1796
01:45:03,000 --> 01:45:05,600
Granny, darling!

1797
01:45:07,800 --> 01:45:10,400
Oh I'm so glad to see you. What made you come?

1798
01:45:10,600 --> 01:45:12,200
Oh I know when I'm needed.

1799
01:45:13,000 --> 01:45:15,300
Now get out of here! Go on, get out of here, all of you!

1800
01:45:15,300 --> 01:45:17,480
I want to talk to my grand-daughter alone.

1801
01:45:18,000 --> 01:45:20,400
I came just as quickly as i could.

1802
01:45:20,400 --> 01:45:23,100
But I'm going home. I sent you a ride yesterday.

1803
01:45:23,800 --> 01:45:29,200
Hm! Sit down!

1804
01:45:32,880 --> 01:45:34,780
Is it true that you're going to uit the movie?

1805
01:45:35,300 --> 01:45:37,000
I never want to hear them again.

1806
01:45:39,800 --> 01:45:41,400
What''re you running away from?

1807
01:45:42,800 --> 01:45:44,700
I'm not running away.

1808
01:45:45,210 --> 01:45:47,840
It's just that i can't go on. My heart isn't there anymore.

1809
01:45:49,400 --> 01:45:53,400
Once i told you to get what you want to have to give your heart in exchange

1810
01:45:54,200 --> 01:45:56,900
And you said, you will win, remember?

1811
01:45:57,360 --> 01:45:58,810
I remember.

1812
01:46:00,400 --> 01:46:03,000
Seems to me that you got more than you were bargaining for.

1813
01:46:03,030 --> 01:46:07,730
More fame. More success. Even more, personal happiness.

1814
01:46:08,100 --> 01:46:10,900
Maybe, more unhappiness.

1815
01:46:11,500 --> 01:46:17,310
But you did make a bargain and now you're whining over it.

1816
01:46:17,310 --> 01:46:21,430
I don't think i'd feel so very proud of myself, if i were you

1817
01:46:22,100 --> 01:46:25,200
I'm not Granny, my mind's made up

1818
01:46:28,800 --> 01:46:31,700
But Granny-
I was proud of you Esther.

1819
01:46:31,750 --> 01:46:35,600
I was proud to be the grandmother of Esther.

1820
01:46:35,600 --> 01:46:40,800
It gave me something to live for. Now, I haven't any.

1821
01:46:42,360 --> 01:46:48,250
I know. i wanna be strong but I can't go on.

1822
01:46:49,100 --> 01:46:53,100
You must. Tragedy is a test of courage.

1823
01:46:53,200 --> 01:46:56,900
If you can meet it bravely, it will leave you bigger than it found you.

1824
01:46:57,040 --> 01:47:00,910
If not, then you'll have to live all your life as a coward.

1825
01:47:01,080 --> 01:47:05,530
Because no matter where you may run, you can never run away from yourself.

1826
01:47:07,400 --> 01:47:09,930
I never knew Norman Maine.

1827
01:47:10,150 --> 01:47:13,310
He wrote me a very sweet letter when you were married.

1828
01:47:13,660 --> 01:47:17,210
He said you told him how much I meant to you.

1829
01:47:17,380 --> 01:47:21,720
And I know just how much you must meant to him.

1830
01:47:22,800 --> 01:47:27,090
You know, Esther. I can't believe that whenever you meet

1831
01:47:27,300 --> 01:47:29,000
he can be very happy

1832
01:47:29,030 --> 01:47:34,760
knowing that he just broke the spirit of the little girl he praised me so highly

1833
01:47:34,830 --> 01:47:38,300
and I can't believe that he can be very proud

1834
01:47:38,390 --> 01:47:42,620
knowing that all the great love is to you

1835
01:47:42,800 --> 01:47:46,400
was to make me a Putter.

1836
01:47:48,220 --> 01:47:52,450
The car is ready Ms. Esther. You'll have to go now to make the train.

1837
01:47:58,020 --> 01:48:00,920
Put the car back in the garage.

1838
01:48:22,800 --> 01:48:25,280
The girl who has won the heart of Hollywood.

1839
01:48:25,330 --> 01:48:27,370
The girl who has won the heart of the world.

1840
01:48:27,410 --> 01:48:29,240
Ms. Vicki Lester!

1841
01:48:29,300 --> 01:48:34,080
And if i'm not mistaken, Ms. Lester's car has just been arrived.

1842
01:48:34,200 --> 01:48:36,700
Yes, it is here!

1843
01:48:52,600 --> 01:48:54,700
I scare very slowly, young man!

1844
01:48:54,720 --> 01:48:56,480
smile folks, please!

1845
01:49:01,720 --> 01:49:03,650
They'll have your mug-

1846
01:49:03,720 --> 01:49:08,300
I mean, your face plastered across half
the papers in the country tomorrow
- Hm how do i look?

1847
01:49:08,360 --> 01:49:10,410
Oh you look well

1848
01:49:10,500 --> 01:49:12,200
You're a liar, but I like ya.

1849
01:49:12,210 --> 01:49:13,980
and here Mrs.Lester Grandmother.

1850
01:49:14,490 --> 01:49:16,600
Would you say a few words to the radio audience, please?

1851
01:49:17,060 --> 01:49:18,260
Say something, lady

1852
01:49:18,900 --> 01:49:24,900
You know, we've got a thing like that back home where they all listen
You know but we call it a pathy-line

1853
01:49:25,000 --> 01:49:25,800
(Laughing)

1854
01:49:25,810 --> 01:49:28,240
Won't you say something, please? They're listening.

1855
01:49:30,660 --> 01:49:35,040
Maybe some of you people listening
in dream about coming to Hollywood.

1856
01:49:35,090 --> 01:49:38,170
Maybe some of you will get pretty discouraged.

1857
01:49:38,570 --> 01:49:42,180
Well, when you do, you just think about me.

1858
01:49:42,490 --> 01:49:44,450
It took me over seventy—

1859
01:49:45,330 --> 01:49:49,420
sixty years to get here, but here I am

1860
01:49:49,620 --> 01:49:51,750
and here I mean to stay.

1861
01:49:52,000 --> 01:49:54,500
(Applause and laughing)
Thank you very much.

1862
01:49:59,100 --> 01:50:00,800
Norman Maine
GOOD LUCK

1863
01:50:05,690 --> 01:50:07,250
Miss Lester

1864
01:50:11,500 --> 01:50:13,600
This microphone is on an international hookup.

1865
01:50:13,700 --> 01:50:16,800
Throughout the world, your fans are hoping
that you'll say a few words to them.

1866
01:50:25,080 --> 01:50:26,630
Hello, everyone...

1867
01:50:27,260 --> 01:50:30,650
This is Mrs. Norman Maine.

1868
01:50:36,500 --> 01:50:39,200
ESTHER AT MICROPHONE

1869
01:50:39,200 --> 01:50:42,300
Esther
Hello, everybody... This is Mrs. Norman Maine.

1870
01:50:42,400 --> 01:50:45,600
The ovation is tremendous.
CAMERA MOVES IN TO BIG CLOSEUP OF ESTHER.

1871
01:50:45,700 --> 01:50:48,900
Tears are starting down her cheeks.
She looks out past all this crowd, this confusion,

1872
01:50:49,000 --> 01:50:52,100
this uproar, to some distant point of her own.
The music swells up.
FADE OUT.

1873
01:50:52,200 --> 01:50:53,200
THE END.

1874
01:50:53,330 --> 01:50:55,640
A Selznick International Picture

1875
01:50:58,703 --> 01:51:05,709
FROM THE COLLECTION OF
GEORGE EASTMAN HOUSE
